The decision, which government officials framed as â€œnecessary to preserve domestic food security,â€ comes as the country faces its worst drought in decadesâ€”drying up reservoirs, shrinking harvests, and forcing a reckoning with the fragile hydrological underpinnings of modern agriculture.

