Thailand is the worldâ€™s second-largest exporter of rice, after India, and a cornerstone of food supply for countries from Indonesia to Nigeria.
But the Chao Phraya River Basinâ€”the engine of Thai agricultureâ€”is running dry.
According to the Thai Meteorological Department, rainfall this season is 35% below normal.
Reservoirs like Bhumibol and Sirikit, which feed the vast rice paddies of the central plains, are nearing "dead storage" levels.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;H10bRUrKilQtOcpNRDPu9wqZPXduGPQVbonQeoXX3Esƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bhyO0Jc92kmL4kXO41_KTMyJko92WJbD-5rJ64E7zwMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkb46ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Uyb5R2uTH4tZ4LxI-YzxpIy27fOJl-_-HnLKhMqeb1Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bb2TUHYybFlRgAHSP5YrMdY35RCnU8OxeXNTj-ccTPwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgChicagoƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DsTiK0_MM2Er2_eMgLcftf0jldHrBGlQ08oErOonTnwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyItâ€™s caloric stability for over half the global population.
When Thailand stops exporting, the shockwave travels instantly.
In just 24 hours, rice futures on the Chicago Mercantile Exchange spiked 18%, with Indian exporters now overwhelmed by new orders.
Countries like the Philippines and Senegalâ€”already facing price volatilityâ€”have begun emergency procurement negotiations.
